      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y={log}_{3}⁡(5x-2).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={log}_{3}⁡(u),\\,u=5x-2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{(5x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
